/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An LED is a forward-biased p-n junction.
   - A planar LED is the simplest surface emitter.
   - A higher-radiance surface emitter, called a Burrus LED in some syllabi, uses a small active area and a substrate window or well so light leaves from one face.
   - Four efficiencies are easy to mix.
   - ηint = Nph(int)/Ne, ηext = ηint × ηextraction, ηpower = Pout/(I V)
   - Nph(int) is photons generated per second; Ne is electrons injected per second; Pout is escaping optical power in W; I is forward current in A; V is forward voltage in V. ηpower is wall-plug power efficiency.
   - Power efficiency includes electrical losses, so it is lower than ηext.
   - Pout = ηext (I/e) E, dPout/dI = ηext E/e
   - E is photon energy in J; e = 1.60×10⁻¹⁹ C is the electron charge.
   - Linear power against current lets you modulate an LED by changing its drive current.
*/
